    "params": {
        "video_subject": "5 · The red sneakers effect",
        "video_script": "Number five: the red sneakers effect. And stay for number seven: it's a single question, and most people's answer surprises them. Researchers went to luxury boutiques in Milan, the kind of shops with a doorman. They described two shoppers to the shop assistants. One walked in wearing a dress and a fur coat. The other walked in wearing gym clothes. Which one did the assistants think was the more important customer, more likely to buy? The one in gym clothes. In another study, students rated a professor at a top university. Described wearing red sneakers instead of formal shoes, he was judged as higher status and more competent. When someone breaks a dress code on purpose, your brain makes a quick calculation: they must have enough status to not care. Think of the founder in a hoodie at a board meeting. Your brain doesn't read sloppy. It reads: this person doesn't need to impress anyone. But there's a limit. It only worked when the rule-breaking looked deliberate, in a setting where people expected the rules. A professor in red sneakers at a top university: status. A random mismatched outfit: just a mistake. Try this today: you don't have to dress like money to be taken seriously. A confident, deliberate choice sends a stronger signal than a label. Which leads to the study that changed how researchers think about fake luxury.",
